    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The fact that pseudotachylytes are rare along exhumed fault zones suggests that frictional melting may not play a major role in most unstable fault slip. However, heat generation has been identified as a viable mechanism for transitioning between stable and unstable sliding due primarily to associated rises in pore pressure. A number of models have been proposed to relate heat generation due to frictional sliding to dynamic fault weakening, but little work has been done to test these models on real faults. I propose to use an existing one-dimensional geophysical model to characterize the effects of spatial variations in fault zone structural and hydraulic properties. Quantitative model results will be coupled with qualitative evidence of slip behavior to characterize paleo-slip physics on faults exhumed from seismogenic depths.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
